Careful: `or` on bools — C++ `||` fine; use `||`. But short-circuit: if tail_h_b.need returns true, tail_p_b.need would NOT run with `||`! Both allocations must happen! Use `|` (both evaluated) or separate ifs. Separate ifs are clearest... but then the reset condition spans two calls. Use:

```cpp
    const bool tail_new = tail_h_b.need(window * row_h) | tail_p_b.need(window * row_p);
    const bool fresh_new = fresh_h_b.need((n0 + 1) * row_h) | fresh_p_b.need((n0 + 1) * row_p);
    if (tail_new) tail_filled = 0;
    if (fresh_new) fresh_filled = 0;
```

`|` on bools evaluates both ✓.

And poison pos_k: after the pos alloc block, `memset(pos_k, 0xAA, n * 8);`.

Also the out buffers' `need` return values unused — fine (ignore with (void) or just call). Calls to need returning bool discarded — fine in C++.

